@PACK Campfire Cooking
@ENTRY coals|Cooking on Coals, Not Flames
The single biggest mistake in campfire cooking is trying to cook over flames. Flames are for making coals; coals are for cooking. Light your fire 30 to 45 minutes before you want to eat so it burns down to a thick, even bed of glowing embers.

Good cooking coals glow orange under a light coat of gray ash and give steady heat with no leaping flame. Rake them into a flat bed with a stick. For a simple heat check, hold your palm where the food will sit: if you can hold it there about 3 to 4 seconds it is medium, 2 seconds is hot, 5 or more is low and slow.

Build your fire off to one side and keep pushing fresh coals from the flames over to a separate cooking area. That way you always have new coals coming without smothering your food in smoke.
@END
@ENTRY foilpackets|Foil Packet Dinners
Foil packets are the easiest hot meal at camp: no pots to scrub, and everyone builds their own. Tear off a large sheet of heavy-duty foil, or double up regular foil, and pile food in the center.

A classic combo: sliced potatoes, baby carrots, and a scoop of frozen or canned corn, topped with a pat of butter, salt, pepper, and a little garlic powder. Add pre-cooked sausage or seasoned ground beef if you want protein. Cheese goes on at the very end.

Fold the foil into a sealed pouch with room for steam, seam-side up. Set it on a bed of medium coals for 15 to 20 minutes, turning once. Open carefully, the steam is hot. Everything steams in its own juices and comes out tender.
@END
@ENTRY skewers|Skewers and Sticks
A sharpened green stick or a metal skewer turns coals into a grill. Green hardwood sticks (never resinous pine) about thumb-thick won't burn through before the food is done.

Cut meat and firm vegetables into even chunks so they cook at the same rate. Onions, peppers, mushrooms, and zucchini all skewer well. Precook dense items like potato near the coals first, then skewer to finish.

Hold or prop skewers a few inches above the coals and turn often. The goal is a slow char, not a quick burn. For dessert, toast bread dough wrapped around a stick, or the classic marshmallow: patience over low coals beats a flaming torch every time.
@END
@ENTRY grill|Grilling Over a Grate
A folding grate laid over rocks or a fire ring turns your coal bed into a proper grill. Let the grate heat for a few minutes and oil it lightly so food releases cleanly.

Burgers, sausages, chicken thighs, and firm fish all grill well. Keep a hot zone (thick coals) and a cool zone (thin coals) so you can sear over the hot side, then move food to the cool side to finish without burning. Chicken especially needs the cool-side finish to cook through safely.

Meat is done by temperature, not color: poultry to 165F, ground meat to 160F, whole cuts of beef or pork to at least 145F with a short rest. A cheap probe thermometer is worth its weight in the backcountry.
@END
@ENTRY oneskillet|One-Skillet Camp Meals
A cast-iron skillet on a grate or a flat rock in the coals handles breakfast, dinner, and everything between. Preheat it, add a little oil, and treat it like a stove burner set to medium.

A camp hash: diced precooked potatoes crisped in oil, then add cooked sausage or ham and a handful of shredded cheese, and finish by cracking eggs on top and covering with foil until set. Feeds a crowd from one pan.

For dinner, brown ground beef, stir in a can of drained beans, a can of diced tomatoes, and taco seasoning, and simmer a few minutes. Scoop into tortillas or over corn chips. Wipe the skillet hot with a paper towel and a splash of water to clean, and re-oil before storing.
@END
@ENTRY safety|Fire Safety and Cleanup
Never cook over a fire you can't control or leave. Keep water and a shovel within arm's reach the entire time. Check local fire rules and bans before you strike a match; many areas restrict open fires in dry months.

Keep raw meat separate from ready-to-eat food and wash hands or use sanitizer before handling food. Coolers should hold 40F or below, and cooked food shouldn't sit out more than two hours (one hour if it's hot out).

When you're done, let the fire burn down, then douse it with water, stir the ashes, and douse again until it is cold to the touch. Pack out all foil and trash, food scraps included, or you'll train wildlife and lose the privilege of fires for everyone.
@END
